.TH SENSORPULSE 1 "November 2026" "SensorPulse @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sensorpulse \- sensor monitor with visual and sound alarms
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sensorpulse
[\fB\-\-background\fR | \fB\-\-window\fR | \fB\-\-settings\fR | \fB\-\-wizard\fR | \fB\-\-detail\fR \fIID\fR]
.br
.B sensorpulse status
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B sensorpulse report
.br
.B sensorpulse export
\fIID\fR [\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows CPU, GPU, disk, fan and battery sensors in the panel, in a window or floating on the
desktop. Alarms know the design limit of the processor and warn when it slows down because
of heat, when it gets hot while almost idle or when a fan stops. It only reads sensors and
never needs root to run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-background
Start in the background (panel and floating widget only).
.TP
.B \-\-window
Open the main window.
.TP
.B \-\-settings
Open the settings.
.TP
.B \-\-wizard
Run the welcome assistant again.
.TP
.BI \-\-detail " ID"
Open the window on the detail of sensor \fIID\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-rename " ID"
Give sensor \fIID\fR a name of your own (used by the panel applet, the GNOME extension and the
desktop widget).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Current values as text, or as JSON with \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.B report
Anonymous hardware report in Markdown (no computer name, user, serial numbers or addresses).
.TP
.B export
History of one sensor as CSV, or JSON with \fB\-\-json\fR (default: last day).
\fIN\fR must be greater than 0 (up to 3650). Exit status: 0 on success, 1 if the service is not
available, 2 for invalid arguments, 3 if the sensor does not exist.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sensorpulse/config.json
Settings. The last good copy is kept as \fIconfig.json.bak\fR; an unreadable file is kept as
\fIconfig.json.corrupt\-DATE\fR and never overwritten.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sensorpulse/history.sqlite
History (1-minute averages for 7 days, 10-minute averages for 30 days) and alarm log.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sensorpulse-service (1)
